WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.850
Research fraud refers to cases where data
were either fabricated or falsified to provide

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:17.020
false support for the researcher's hypothesis.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:21.490
Fabrication means the data were never collected,
but were made up.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:26.390
Falsification means existing data were illegitimately
altered including manipulating research materials,

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:30.910
images, data, equipment, or processes.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:35.570
Falsification also includes changing or omitting
data or results in such a way that the research

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:38.190
is not accurately represented.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:42.890
For example, a person might falsify data to
make it fit with the desired end result of

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:46.690
a study.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:52.339
In 1998, physician Andrew Wakefield published
a study in The Lancet, claiming that his research

00:00:52.339 --> 00:00:57.379
indicated a connection between autism and
the measles-mumps-rubella vaccine.

00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:01.969
This research was highly respected and undermined
public confidence in the vaccine, leading

00:01:01.969 --> 00:01:04.550
to many parents refusing the shot.

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:09.560
Ultimately, this led to increases in the number
of cases of measles and mumps in the U.S.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.800
and Europe, with some areas reporting very
dangerous and widespread outbreaks.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.860
When faced with an investigation in 2010,
it was revealed that Wakefield and his colleagues

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.030
had altered facts about the children in their
study, and Wakefield had even been paid off

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.940
by a lawyer planning to sue the manufacturer
of the vaccine.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:36.240
The British General Medical Council found
Wakefield guilty of fraud and misconduct.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.320
In 2006, Korean researcher Hwang Woo-suk was
found to have fabricated a series of experiments

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.850
in stem cell research, a field in which he
was once considered one of the pioneering

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.300
experts.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.730
He was previously famous for his two Science
journal articles in which he reported success

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:56.460
in creating human embryonic stem cells through
cloning, but is now infamous for his massive

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:01.031
case of fraud and scientific misconduct after
it was revealed that much of his stem cell

00:02:01.031 --> 00:02:03.120
research had been faked.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.119
Hwang was charged with embezzlement and bioethics
law violations, for which he was sentenced

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:12.370
to a two-year suspended prison sentence and
barred from engaging in stem cell research

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:16.810
by the South Korean government, as well as
fired from his position with Seoul National

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:17.810
University.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:23.120
However, Hwang continues to lead research
in creating embryonic stem cell lines from

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:31.280
cloned pig embryos, and his lab has been actively
publishing manuscripts on PubMed.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:37.960
In January 2014, Haruko Obokata from the Riken
Center for Developmental Biology in Kobe,

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:43.280
Japan, published 2 papers describing the method,
called stimulus-triggered acquisition of pluripotency

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:44.950
in Nature.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:49.390
But they were retracted in July after Nature
discovered that the papers included plagiarized

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:53.650
writing, misidentified images, and misreported
data.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:58.590
Haruko Obokata, the lead author, who performed
the studies and wrote the manuscripts, was

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.810
found guilty of falsifying data.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:07.850
Biologist Yoshiki Sasai, leader of the Riken
research group, was a co-author on both papers.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:11.130
He was criticized for his poor supervision.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:17.069
During the investigation, Sasai committed
suicide at the Riken Center.

00:03:17.069 --> 00:03:22.270
In 2016, a group of researchers from University
Malaya was found to duplicate the images of

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:25.980
cells in the photos contained in their research
article.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:31.050
The researchers were later found to have published
the same results 3 times, in three different

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.220
journals.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.930
The authors are almost all from Malaysia,
and not the same on these three publications.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:42.810
Yet, all three papers have same two corresponding
authors Sekaran Muniandy, a professor at the

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:48.130
Department Of Molecular Medicine at the University
of Malaya in Kuala Lumpur, and the first author,

00:03:48.130 --> 00:04:31.570
his PhD student, Nima Samie.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:05:16.530
Overall, academic fraud is rare, which makes
it all the more shocking when a major case

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:18.340
is uncovered.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:24.090
To the public, it may seem mind-boggling that
scientists would go to such lengths to deceive.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:28.560
In an ideal world, scientists work together
to make incremental discoveries that add to

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.150
the body of knowledge in a field and are recognized
for quality work.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:38.810
In reality, the world of science can be cutthroat
and isolating, with little oversight.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:43.070
Stem cell research is certainly not the only
research field with a fraud problem, but it

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:45.310
has all the right elements to motivate dishonesty.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:51.820
It’s a cutting-edge field with the potential
to discover treatments for human diseases.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:57.280
It attracts highly competitive people who
are all scrambling to make the next big discovery.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.610
And that discovery must be made, written,
and published before any competitors can catch

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:02.919
up.

00:06:02.919 --> 00:06:07.210
Add that to an academic culture that places
ever-rising pressure on researchers to churn

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:12.479
out publications in order to land jobs or
tenure, especially publications in high-impact

00:06:12.479 --> 00:06:16.889
journals like Nature and Science, and you
begin to see why researchers resort to cutting

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:22.400
corners or massaging their data.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.780
In 2014, Nobel Prize winner Randy Schekman
spoke out against publishing in prestigious

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.950
journals, saying that they contributed to
unhealthy research practices, and advised

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:34.240
people to boycott them.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:39.200
Barring acceptance at a major journal, academics
must still publish somewhere, and this pressure

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:43.580
has created a market for yet another type
of academic fraud, predatory journals.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:44.580
Both fabrication and falsification are serious
forms of misconduct because they result in

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:45.580
a scientific record that does not accurately
reflect observed truth.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:46.580
First of all, the scientific literature is
contaminated with false empirical data.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:47.580
This alone holds back scientific progress.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:48.580
Researchers might pursue a line of research
that seems promising, but is in fact baseless.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:49.580
Honest researchers see their projects fail
to replicate promising results.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:50.580
They might incorrectly conclude this is due
to their personal lack of research skills.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:51.580
The project might be abandoned without the
failed replications ever coming to light.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:52.580
Secondly, precious funding resources that
could have been spent on valid avenues of

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:53.580
research are spent on fraudulent research
and related projects that build on it, but

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:54.580
are in fact less promising then they seem.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:55.580
Thirdly, once fraud is exposed, the reputation
and credibility of the field, including the

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:56.580
majority of researchers who do have integrity,
is severely damaged.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:57.580
This can result in more difficulty to obtain
funding in a general area where fraud has

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:58.580
been exposed.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:06:59.580
This puts honest researchers who had the bad
luck to be in a related field at a disadvantage.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:00.580
Fraud cases are invariably accompanied by
reluctance or even unwillingness to share

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:01.580
data and research information with others.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:02.580
Unless researchers are challenged or even
required to be open and transparent, fraud

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:03.650
can be tempting in an academic climate where
publishing positive, confirmatory results

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:06.190
is held in high regard.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:09.639
Certain instances of fraud can be easy to
spot.

00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:14.050
For example, if a referee knows for a fact
that a particular laboratory does not have

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:17.460
the facilities to conduct the research that
was published.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:23.740
Or, if it's obvious an image looks manipulated
or is made up from several different experiments.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:27.830
The data from the control experiments might
be "too perfect".

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:32.440
In such situations, an investigation would
be conducted to determine if an act of fraud

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.110
was committed.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:38.700
To help prevent fraud, most publishers have
strict policies on manipulation of images

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:43.750
and access to the reported data.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:47.220
In general, digital image enhancement is acceptable.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:52.380
However, a positive relationship between the
original data and the resulting image must

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:58.370
be maintained to avoid creating unrepresentative
data or the loss of meaningful signals.

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:04.680
No specific feature within an image may be
enhanced, obscured, moved, removed, or introduced.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:09.590
Adjustments of brightness, contrast, or color
balance are usually acceptable as long as

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:14.570
they do not obscure or eliminate any information
present in the original.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:19.009
If a figure has been significantly manipulated,
you must note the nature of the enhancements

00:08:19.009 --> 00:08:23.220
in the figure legend or in the 'Materials
and Methods' section.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:27.539
Authors may also be asked to provide the raw
data in connection with a paper for editorial

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:28.630
review.

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:35.440
Therefore, all data for a specific paper should
be retained for a reasonable time after publication.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:38.760
There should be named custodian for the data.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:43.399
Studies undertaken in human beings, e.g. clinical
trials have specific guidelines about the

00:08:43.399 --> 00:08:50.149
duration of data retention.

00:08:50.149 --> 00:08:54.680
What about unintentional error that comes
across as misconduct?

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:59.440
According to the U.S. Office of Research Integrity,
research misconduct does not include honest

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.649
error or differences of opinion.

00:09:01.649 --> 00:09:06.780
But, it's best never to have the integrity
of your work come into question.

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:11.030
As a researcher and author, it is essential
to understand what constitutes appropriate

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:16.350
data management, including data collection,
retention, analysis and reporting, in accordance

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:18.329
with responsible conduct of research.

